# nvn/vertex: upstream's "bind the mapped bytes a draw reads" (nxnative c96c45e9 + 79555b67), plus iBuilder's safety net: a stream whose read range cannot be proven is bound up
# to its pool's end instead of having its draw skipped
#
# Replaces the first engine-patches/0506 (nvn-vbuf-clamp-to-pool-end). That one ran South Park on the owner's iPhone 15 Pro, and was the base of 0507-0509.
# Upstream:  https://github.com/seedds/nxnative commits c96c45e9b010d90675903001068a43365ada51ab "nvn/vertex: bind the mapped bytes a draw reads" and
#            79555b67629b285bb2aecfc00b9938d28662dd78 "nvn/vertex: bind the mapped bytes of indexed draws" (2026-11-21, seedds; MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 seedds and
#            contributors, the attribution kept as the licence asks). Taken: their dm/gfx/nvn and dm/gfx/tests parts, which apply to our tree unchanged.
#            Not taken: the README / docs lines of 79555b67, and the commit between them (e74c3fa7, PR #843: the South Park game table, Snowdrop cache decoding, allocator
#            patches) — it changes the shader chain and is not about vertex streams.
# WHY: Snowdrop (South Park: The Fractured But Whole, 01008F2005154000) binds vertex buffers from offsets inside a buffer with the whole buffer's size. The declared range runs past
#   the pool's end, the host's full-range lookup failed, and the draws were skipped: blank character preview, missing loading indicator, the title flickering, frames with no draw.
# WHAT IT CHANGES (dm/gfx/nvn/nvn_mtl.m, nvn_feedback.inc, nvn_geometry.inc, nvn_tess.inc, hle_nvn.c; tests):
#   * Upstream:
#     - bind_vbufs and pull_layout_fill take the draw (dw / di). When the full lookup fails, vbuf_draw_bytes proves the bytes this draw reads: its vertex and instance ranges, or
#       the min / max of a direct indexed draw's CPU-readable indices plus base vertex (vbuf_index_last). Not when the indices have pending writes or the pools are sparse.
#     - Those bytes are bound (not cached) and also bound format conversion (g_vbuf_prefix_mask / g_vbuf_prefix_bytes).
#     - The cache key includes the size.
#     - Unknown or out-of-pool ranges are rejected.
#   * Ours (the fallback): vbuf_fallback_bytes. When the proof gives no range (indirect or multi-draws, pending index writes, a negative effective index, sparse pools, a format of
#     unknown size, an unaligned stream) and the stream's START lies in a pool, the stream is bound up to that pool's end; format conversion is bounded the same way.
#     A range PROVEN to reach past the pool stays skipped, as upstream: a fetch past the buffer would fault.
#   * DM_NVN_VBUF_CLAMP:
#     - 1 (default): the fallback;
#     - 0: no fallback, upstream exactly;
#     - 2: also bind up to the pool's end when the proof says the draw reads past it (the first 0506's behaviour).
#   * Logged, counted, at 1, 2, 4, 8, ...:
#     - "vertex stream S at A: bound the bytes the draw reads (upstream): N of M declared bytes (K so far)";
#     - "... bound up to the pool's end (fallback): ...".
#     Skipped draws: vbuf_skip_note replaces RED_ONCE 158 at its three call sites. It keeps the "is in no memory pool; draw skipped" wording, and adds the size, whether the start
#     is in a pool, the nearest pools and a running count. mtl_vbuf_skips feeds the slow-frame row @vbuf_skips (hle_nvn.c, weak reference).
# DEFAULT: the fallback on.
# Shader chain: not touched (b24b25240cad; dmsh_chain.h unchanged).
# Tests (M1 Pro):
#   - t_vbuf: upstream's, 143 checks, 0 failed in both runs. check_t_vbuf now runs it with DM_NVN_VBUF_CLAMP=0: its expectations are upstream's. With the fallback, its 4 unproven
#     cases (indirect draw, negative index, pending index copy, twice) are bound instead of skipped, as intended.
#   - t_pull: upstream's call-site change, 434/0.
#   - t_vbuf_clamp: rewritten for the fallback, 11/0 in modes 1, 0 and 2 (make check_t_vbuf_clamp).
#   NOT run on a device in this form; no host dylib built.
# 0507, 0508 and 0509 do not apply to this patch without fuzz: rebased copies (context only, same behaviour) are target/agent-work/southpark/rebased-050[789]-*.patch.
# Checked: base + this + the three rebased, --fuzz=0, gives the tested tree byte for byte; paches/0007 -> 0008 still apply on top; dmsh_chain.py prints b24b25240cad.

